1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,279
Your API can create orders and read users.
Then someone asks, "Who is allowed to call
this?" Security is not a controller if you
remember to paste.

2
00:00:09,399 --> 00:00:22,439
In Spring, security is a filter chain —
requests pass through filters that
authenticate and authorize before your
controller runs. Authentication asks who
you are. Authorization asks what you can
do.

3
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:35,639
Never invent crypto for passwords or
tokens when maintained libraries exist.
Look at this java for a moment. A
SecurityFilterChain bean defines the
rules. Permit health checks for
orchestrators.

4
00:00:35,759 --> 00:00:50,519
Require authentication elsewhere. Prefer
readable matchers over clever regex.
Prefer least privilege defaults — deny by
default, permit explicitly — when the
application's risk profile warrants it.

5
00:00:50,639 --> 00:01:04,839
Picture a practical breach path. The SPA
hides admin buttons. A curious user calls
/api/admin/refunds with a stolen session
cookie or token. If authorization lives
only in the UI, the API complies.

6
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:13,999
Server-side authorization in the filter
chain or method security is the real door
lock. Disabling security "temporarily"
forever is how staging configurations
reach production.

7
00:01:14,119 --> 00:01:20,559
Authorize checks only on the UI is how raw
API clients bypass your SPA's pretty
buttons.

8
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:31,879
Password encoders hash credentials
correctly — plaintext passwords are not a
"temporary shortcut," they are a breach
waiting for a calendar date. Use
framework-supported encoder factories.

9
00:01:31,999 --> 00:01:38,359
Never roll a custom hash "for simplicity."
Rotate and migrate encodings when
algorithms age.

10
00:01:38,479 --> 00:01:46,319
Never invent crypto also covers tokens and
cookies — prefer maintained JWT libraries
or session mechanisms your platform
supports.

11
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:57,959
CSRF protection matters for browser
session cookie flows; APIs using tokens
have a different threat model. Understand
your clients before copying a snippet that
disables CSRF.

12
00:01:58,079 --> 00:02:08,279
Disabling security filters "so we can
finish the demo" is how demo config
becomes production config through a
forgotten profile. Least privilege shows
up in role design.

13
00:02:08,399 --> 00:02:18,719
ROLE_USER versus ROLE_FINANCE versus
ROLE_ADMIN should map to real
capabilities. Broad admin for everyone is
shared blast radius.

14
00:02:18,839 --> 00:02:25,639
Method security annotations can complement
the filter chain for deeper checks —
whether this user owns this order id.

15
00:02:25,759 --> 00:02:33,199
Filters decide whether you are
authenticated at all; method security can
decide whether this particular action on
this resource is allowed.

16
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:47,159
Duplicating contradictory rules in filter
matchers and method annotations creates
gaps. AuthN versus AuthZ remains the
interview staple. Authentication: who are
you? Authorization: what can you do?

17
00:02:47,339 --> 00:02:53,899
Keep them distinct — then mention filter
chains and method security as enforcement
points so the answer is operational.

18
00:02:54,019 --> 00:03:02,699
In incident language: authentication
failures mean we do not know who you are;
authorization failures mean we know who
you are and you may not do this.

19
00:03:02,819 --> 00:03:13,459
Mixing those in logs and HTTP statuses
confuses users and on-call engineers.
Build a minimal checklist before merge. Is
authentication happening at the edge?

20
00:03:13,639 --> 00:03:27,799
Are authorization rules enforced on the
server for every sensitive route? Are
passwords encoded with a modern encoder?
Is CSRF configured for the actual client
type? Are Actuator and admin endpoints
locked down?

21
00:03:27,979 --> 00:03:36,699
Did anyone disable security in a profile
that could be active in production? Also
reconnect to testing: security rules
without tests drift.

22
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:48,459
Anonymous access tests and forbidden-role
tests belong beside happy-path controller
tests. You can wire secure APIs and still
ship regressions if tests are slow, flaky,
or missing.

23
00:03:48,579 --> 00:03:54,339
The next pressure is Spring testing —
slices before full context, and realism
when you need it.
